Well, ruminating about the "few cents' worth" of ideas I posted and sent
to the dev team address yesterday, I had some followup notions (an idle
brain is a terrible thing to waste, eh?)...

I had suggested a new class of character: the alchemist, whose special
ability would be to always know the true identity of potions and gems.
This character would introduce into the game a new trick: combining
potions with other potions or with gems, to make different potions
(alchemy). I was thinking about how this could work:

Any character could do it, but not knowing exactly what the ingerdients
were would have perils. For dropping gems into potions:
 - any uncursed worthless piece of glass would simply have a decent
chance (say 1/4) to bless a potion.
 - different gems would change different potions in specific ways (like
a blue gem dropped into a potion of sense food creates a potion of extra
healing, or whatever), which would not change, and would therefore soon
show up in spoiler files. However, if you drop a gem into the wrong
potion, it turns the potion into plain water. The gem is dissolved
regardless. If _either_ the gem or the potion is cursed, then the result
is an explosion which destroys both, blinds and stuns you, does 2-12 hp
of damage to you, and does whatever vapor effects the potion would do.

For combining two potions (maybe needs a new #p(our) command):
 - same deal as far as curses: if either is cursed, you get the
explosion.
 - certain combos create certain potions, others just create a potion of
uncursed water. The two original potions are obviously lost regardless.

There  could be a new tool: the beaker.
 - if you pour your two potions into an uncursed beaker instead of each other,
you don'[t get an explosion if one is cursed. If the beaker is cursed,
you _always_ get the explosion whether or not a potion was cursed, and
the beaker is destroyed. If the beaker is blessed, it identifies the
results of your pouring for you. The magic beaker follows the same
rules, except that it will produce a (random) new potion even if you
combine two potions which do not normally produce anything but water.
All potions created in a magic beaker are blessed.

The alchemist, of course, would start out with a beaker, though not
necessarily a magic one.


I had also suggested a character called the lion tamer. I would add that
a new tool, the whip (distinct from the bullwhip weapon) could be added:
when applied toward a monster that is within two spaces of you, it
causes the beast to shy away for 1-6 turns: not attack, and move away if
it can. This wouldn't apply to @'s or &'s. A magic whip would have this
effect for 3-18 turns, and do a little damage besides. You couldn't
apply this tool of you were wielding a two-handed weapon: you need your
left hand free. Naturally, the lion tamer would start out with a whip.


